\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces (a) The Feynman diagrams contributing at leading-order to the $c-$axis conductivity, $\sigma _c$ (see Eq.~\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {c_axis_cond_terms}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}}), where solid, wiggly and dashed lines denote electrons, spins and phonons, respectively. (b) $c$-axis resistivity as a function of $T$ for different (compressive) $c$-axis strain $\varepsilon _{zz}$ (in units of $\xi /a_z$, where $\xi $ is the characteristic decay length of the hopping integral and $a_z$ is the $\hat  {z}$ lattice constant). Inset: in-plane resistivity as a function of $T$ under $c$-axis strain $\varepsilon _{zz}$. }}{5}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:predictions}{{3}{5}{(a) The Feynman diagrams contributing at leading-order to the $c-$axis conductivity, $\sigma _c$ (see Eq.~\eqref {c_axis_cond_terms}), where solid, wiggly and dashed lines denote electrons, spins and phonons, respectively. (b) $c$-axis resistivity as a function of $T$ for different (compressive) $c$-axis strain $\varepsilon _{zz}$ (in units of $\xi /a_z$, where $\xi $ is the characteristic decay length of the hopping integral and $a_z$ is the $\hat {z}$ lattice constant). Inset: in-plane resistivity as a function of $T$ under $c$-axis strain $\varepsilon _{zz}$}{figure.3}{}}
